5. Commercial Royalty

For every Sold Copy of a Commercial Game, You owe the Licensor a fixed royalty
of CAD $1.00.

The royalty:

a. is calculated per Sold Copy, regardless of retail price;
b. is not reduced by platform commissions, payment-processing fees, taxes,
   marketing expenses, publisher shares, or other costs;
c. applies to direct and indirect sales made by You or on Your behalf,
   including sales through publishers, distributors, stores, and platforms;
   and
d. survives expiration or termination of this License for Sold Copies
   occurring before that expiration or termination.

If a Commercial Game is sold in a bundle, each paid player entitlement to that
Commercial Game counts as one Sold Copy.

The commercial royalty does not transfer ownership of, or grant the Licensor
any license to, Your Game Content. You retain all rights in Game Content,
subject only to this License's terms governing the Licensed Work used by the
Game Project.

6. Quarterly Reports and Payments

Within 30 days after March 31, June 30, September 30, and December 31 of each
year, You must send the Licensor:

a. the Commercial Game's title and applicable storefronts or distribution
   channels;
b. the number of Sold Copies during the quarter, by channel;
c. refunds or chargebacks claimed;
d. cumulative Sold Copies;
e. the royalty calculation in Canadian dollars; and
f. payment of all royalties due.

Reports and requests for payment instructions must be sent to
mmotoobusy@gmail.com. Currency conversion, when necessary, must use the
Bank of Canada daily exchange rate for the date the underlying sale was
settled, or another method agreed to in writing by the Licensor.

Late amounts accrue interest at 1% per month or the maximum lawful rate,
whichever is lower.

7. Records and Verification

You must keep accurate records supporting reports and royalty payments for at
least five years. No more than once per calendar year, the Licensor may, on 15
days' written notice, have an independent professional inspect relevant records
during normal business hours and subject to reasonable confidentiality
obligations.

The Licensor bears the inspection cost unless an underpayment greater than 5%
is found for the inspected period. In that case, You must pay the reasonable
inspection cost in addition to the shortfall and applicable interest.

